===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=playing card|:inh|enm:carde<t:playing card><ety:bor<fro:carte>>}}
From {{inh|en|enm|carde||playing card}}, from {{der|en|fro|carte}}, from {{der|en|la|charta}}, from {{der|en|grc|χάρτης||paper, papyrus}}. {{doublet|en|carte|chart}}.

====Noun====
{{wikidata lexeme|L532}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|flat stiff paper-or-plastic object etc}} Any [[flat]], normally [[rectangular]] piece of [[stiff]] [[paper]], [[plastic]]{{,}} etc.
# {{senseid|en|Q47883}} A [[playing card#English:_Q47883|playing card]].
#* {{RQ:Time|title=Games: Beating the Dealer|date=Jan 25, 1963
|passage=As each '''card''' is played in blackjack, it changes the possibilities for both player and dealer by diminishing the number and the variety of '''cards''' that may be dealt.
|url=https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,940259,00.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250828132227/https://content.time.com/time/subscriber/article/0,33009,940259,00.html|archivedate=28 August 2025}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} Any [[game]] using [[playing card]]s; a [[card game]].
#: {{ux|en|He played '''cards''' with his friends.}}
# A [[resource]] or [[argument]], used to achieve a purpose. {{q|See {{m|en|play the something card}}.}}
#: {{ux|en|The government played the Orange '''card''' to get support for their Ireland policy.}}
#: {{ux|en|He accused them of [[play the race card|playing the race '''card''']].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Luke McNamara|title=Human Rights Controversies: The Impact of Legal Form|page=138|passage=Having adopted civil union as their goal, proponents of the Civil Union Bill were sensitive to the need not to overplay the human rights '''card''', aware that there was a significant degree of resistance in the New Zealand {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=Last Ride of the High Seas Fleet - Battle of Texel 1918|date=17 October 2018|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YrTeYcRXDEw|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220804035059/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YrTeYcRXDEw|archivedate=4 August 2022|accessdate=4 August 2022|time=24:24|text=Realizing he is now boxed in on all sides, Hipper decides the only remaining '''card''' he has to play is to sell his ships as dearly as possible. The remaining German ships make a hard turn southeast, and drive headlong at the Grand Fleet. It is a brave gesture, but only eight of the ships emerge from the pall of smoke that roughly marks the original German line of advance. Two more emerge minutes later, but that is all.}}
# A [[bank card]].
# Any electronic payment (rather than a cash payment using notes, bills or coins).
#: {{ux|en|Cashier: "Cash or '''card'''? Customer: "I'm paying on my phone, so '''card'''."}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} <!-- definitely UK; elsewhere? --> [[paper|Paper]] that is thicker and more durable than normal writing or printing paper, but thinner and more flexible than [[paperboard]], used for postcards, playing cards, etc.; [[card stock]].
#: {{ux|en|Do you have any '''card'''? I want to make a poster.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[map]] or [[chart]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1590|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=w:The Faerie Queene|section=II.vii|passage=As pilot well expert in perilous waue, / Vpon his '''card''' and compas firmes his eye {{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} An [[amusing]] or [[entertaining]] person, often [[slightly]] [[eccentric]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=Siegfried Sassoon|title=s:The General|passage="He's a cheery old '''card'''," muttered Harry to Jack / As they slogged up to Arras with rifle and pack. / . . . / But he did for them both by his plan of attack.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|year=2007|author=w:Meredith Gran|url=http://www.octopuspie.com/2007-11-12/071-deadpan/|title=Octopus Pie #71: Deadpan
|site=octopuspie.com
|passage=MAREK: But really the deadpan is key. You can essentially trick people into laughing at ''nothing''.<br>EVE: Oh, Marek, you '''card'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:Sylvia Plath|title=s:The Bell Jar|publisher=Faber and Faber
|passage='What a '''card'''!' Doreen said. 'Isn't he a '''card'''?'}}
# A list of scheduled events or of performers or contestants; chiefly used in [[professional wrestling]].
#: {{ux|en|What's on the '''card''' for tonight?}}
# {{lb|en|cricket}} A [[tabular]] [[presentation]] of the [[key]] [[statistics]] of an [[innings]] or [[match]]: [[batsman|batsmen]]’s [[score]]s and how they were [[dismissed]], [[extras]], total score and [[bowling figures]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} A removable electronic device that may be inserted into a powered electronic device to provide additional capability.
#: {{syn|en|expansion card}}
#: {{ux|en|He needed to replace the '''card''' his computer used to connect to the internet.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} Any of a set of [[page]]s or [[form]]s that the user can [[navigate]] between, and fill with [[data]], in certain [[user interface]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=Danny Goodman|title=The Complete HyperCard 2.2 Handbook|page=198
|passage=The button will "see" the cursor through a '''card''' domain graphic; you can then change button graphics on each '''card'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Johan Hjelm|title=Why IPTV?: Interactivity, Technologies, Services|page=13
|passage=The interaction model of WAP, originally developed for mobile phones to interact with information services in a web-like way, was based on Apple's HyperCard, and instead of pages, the user interacted with a deck of '''cards''', which were interlinked by a scripting language.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Veljko Milutinovic; Frédéric Patricelli|title=Mastering E-Business Infrastructure|page=329
|passage=The basic building blocks of WML applications are '''cards'''. '''Cards''' are equivalent to HTML pages. Just as Web browser can show only one page at a time (except when using frames), a WAP enabled device can also show only one '''card''' at a time.}}
# A [[greeting card]].
#: {{ux|en|She gave her neighbors a '''card''' congratulating them on their new baby.}}
# A [[business card]].
#: {{ux|en|The realtor gave me her '''card''' so I could call if I had any questions about buying a house.}}
# {{lb|en|television}} A [[title card]] or [[intertitle]]: a piece of filmed, printed text edited into the midst of the photographed action at various points, generally to convey character dialogue or descriptive narrative material related to the plot.
# A [[test card]].
# In [[formal]] [[debate|debating]], a [[verbatim]] [[citation]] used as [[evidence]] for a [[point]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=N. Andre Cossette|title=The Art of Debate: 12th Edition|page=123
|passage=You can make most theory answers without '''cards''', but some '''cards''' do exist which specifically criticize [[kritik]]s on a theoretical basis.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A published note, containing a brief statement, explanation, request, expression of thanks, etc.
#: {{co|en|to put a '''card''' in the newspapers}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A printed [[programme]].
# {{lb|en|dated|figurative|by extension}} An [[attraction]] or [[inducement]].
#: {{ux|en|This will be a good '''card''' for the last day of the fair.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} {{ellipsis of|en|compass card}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1594|author=Richard Hooker|title=Laws of Ecclesiastical Polity|section=1.2.5|page=153 (Everyman edition, vol. 1)|passage="The Lord possessed me [= Wisdom] in the beginning of his way, even before his works of old was I set up;" that law, which hath been the pattern to make, and is the '''card''' to guide the world by}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|I|iii|passage=All the quarters that they know / I' the shipman's '''card'''.}}
# {{lb|en|weaving}} A perforated [[pasteboard]] or sheet-metal plate for [[warp]] [[thread]]s, making part of the Jacquard apparatus of a [[loom]].
# {{lb|en|graph theory}} A [[graph]] formed from a given graph by deleting one [[vertex]].
#: {{cot|en|deck}}
# An [[indicator card]].
# {{lb|en|Philippines|education}} {{ellipsis of|en|report card}}.
#: {{ux|en|Where's your '''card'''? I want to see your grades.}}
# {{lb|en|industrial relations}} A [[form]] signed by an [[employee]] to agree to [[representation]] by a [[union]].

